%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                          Introduction                         %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

(story title) Forensic Necromancy: A Murder in Nineveh
(story author) Daniel M. Stelzer
(story ifid) 185CEA3F-ED51-498B-92D7-C1A62F2D431C
(story noun) A Historical Mystery
(story release 1)
(story blurb)
	The theory holds up, and the lab data is promising. But if you want to be the first PhD in Forensic Necromancy, you need results the world can’t ignore. Which is why you’re now creeping through Nineveh in the dead of night, searching for human remains in situ. What millennia-old secrets are waiting to be unearthed? And most importantly—will they be enough to impress a thesis committee?

%% Required by TALJ rules
(content warnings)
	(div @meta) {
		(span @bold) { Content warning: }
		This game includes significant \( but non-graphic \) discussion of murder and capital punishment, and minor depiction of psychedelics used for ritual purposes. The author considers it “PG” rated. (par)
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click below or press ENTER to continue.
		(else)
			Press any key to continue.
		(endif)
	}
	(pause)
	(clear all)

(intro)
	(content warnings)
	Six years of work, countless hours in the library and the lab, hunched over your laptop, poring over old manuscripts or departmental paperwork...and now it’s almost over. Your advisor has given you a deadline, and you need to defend your thesis by the end of the year, or have nothing to show for it all. (par)
	Your data is promising. The experimental results are fantastic, the p-values are significant, and you’ve gotten a couple publications out of the process. But if you want to be the first PhD in Forensic Necromancy, you need more than lab results. You need something the world can’t ignore. (par)
	Which means you need human remains in situ, and the older the better. The last of your advisor’s grant funding got you to Nineveh just after midnight. The rest is up to you.
	(banner)
	(try [look])

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                            Tutorial                           %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

%% Give people a way to turn it off, but it starts enabled
(tutorial is active)
(understand command [tutorial off])
(perform [tutorial off]) (now) ~(tutorial is active)
(give the tutorial) ~(tutorial is active)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught rope)
	(current focus #rope)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Examining things will often suggest what you might do with them. The rope, for example, is meant to be climbed. But where would that be useful?
	}
	(now) (taught rope)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught moving)
	(#camp is unvisited) %% These are the two rooms adjacent to the start
	(#abovedig is unvisited)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Most text adventures are played by
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			clicking links or
		(endif)
		typing in commands. To move around, for example, type a compass direction: (command link {southwest}) \( (command link {sw}) \) or (command link {south}) \( (command link {s}) \) .
	}
	(now) (taught moving)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught examining)
	(current room $Room)
	*($Obj has ancestor $Room)
	~(current player $Obj)
	(div @tutorial) {
		(color link [links] {Highlighted}) words indicate things you can interact with.
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click the words
		(else)
			Type these words
		(endif)
		to examine them.
	}
	(now) (taught examining)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught items)
	~(taught inventory)
	(current focus $Obj)
	(item $Obj)
	(div @tutorial) {
		When you see a small object, like (the $Obj), you can pick it up and take it with you. Try to (command link {take (the $Obj)}).
	}
	(now) (hinted items)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught interaction)
	(current focus #backpack)
	(suggestions enabled)
	(div @tutorial) {
		The bar above the command prompt suggests some possible commands you could try.
		(if) (#backpack is open) (then)
			If something is open, for example, you might close it;
		(else)
			If something is closed, for example, you might open it;
		(endif)
		(if) (#backpack is #wornby $) (then)
			if you’re wearing something, you might take it off.
		(else)
			if something is wearable, you might put it on.
		(endif)
		But you can always ignore it and type your own commands instead.
	}
	(now) (taught interaction)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught inventory)
	*($Obj is #heldby #p-real)
	~($Obj = #tools)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Quite a lot of text adventure puzzles involve finding objects and moving them around. To see what you’re currently holding, (command link {take inventory}) \( or (command link {i}) for short \) .
	}
	(now) (taught inventory)

(give the tutorial)
	~(taught map)
	(map enabled)
	(#camp is visited) %% At least three visited rooms on the map
	(#abovedig is visited) %% You need to specifically visit these two (plus the starting room) before you can get anywhere else
	(div @tutorial) {
		The map
		(if) (interpreter supports status bar) (then)
			at the top of the screen
		(else)
			above the command prompt
		(endif)
		marks your current location with a (span @command) \@
		(if) (pointers enabled) (then)
			, and any puzzles you’re currently able to solve with a (space) (span @command) !
		(endif)
		.
		(if) (interpreter supports links) (then)
			Click any room to go there.
		(endif)
	}
	(now) (taught map)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%                     The Real World : Above                    %%%%%%%%%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

#nabiyunus
(room *)
(name *) Tell Nabi Yunus
(proper *)
(look *)
	No hiking through the desert for you: the dig site is right in the heart of Mosul, Iraq, the mound known as Prophet Jonah’s Hill. There’s supposed to be a very nice bakery across the street, but it wasn’t open at 2:00 AM; you had to make do with a box of cookies from the supermarket on the way over. (par)
	But you made it.
	(if) (#dig is unvisited) (then)
		They say the field crew came across more bones today, and that’s exactly what you need.
	(else)
		You found the human remains you’re looking for; now all you need is enough data to cap off your thesis.
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #abovedig)
(from * go #southwest to #camp)

#tools
(name *) various tools
(dict *) tool ball compass mirror entheogen entheogens tablet tablets pill pills keychain packet
(plural *)
(your *)
(item *)
(* is #heldby #p-real)
(descr *)
	The tools of a forensic necromancer’s trade: a mirror, a packet of entheogens, and a keychain with a little ball compass. Everything you need to divine memories of the past. (par)
	Well, maybe the compass isn’t strictly necessary. But archaeologists lay everything out by compass directions, so that one helps you find those memories in the first place.

(prevent [drop/put * | $])
	But without your tools, how would you accomplish anything here?

(prevent [take *]) %% Needs to handle both TAKE COMPASS and TAKE PILLS
	You’ve got them all close at hand, ready to use when the moment comes.
	(if) (current room #dig/#looseearth/#deeppit) (then)
		All you have to do now is (link) {divine the bones} .
	(endif)

#camp
(room *)
(name *) camp
(your *)
(room heading *) Your camp
(look *)
	The main field crew keeps their equipment off to the (#south), but you’ve made an unobtrusive little camp here
	(if) (#backpack is pristine) (then)
		: your (#bike) locked to a piece of machinery, your (#backpack) looped over the handlebars.
	(else)
		, with your (#bike) locked to a convenient piece of (zlink {machinery}) .
	(endif)

(from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #northeast to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #east to #abovedig)

#bike
(name *) bicycle
(dict *) bike handlebar handlebars handle bar bars handles
(your *)
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) It’s faster than walking, and more reliable than a cab in the early hours of the morning.
(on-seat *)

(prevent [enter/climb/unlock/open/untie * | $]) (bike refusal)

#machinery
(name *) piece of machinery
(dict *) machine lock
(* is #in #camp)
(descr *) You have no idea what this machine does, but for right now it’s large and immovable.

(prevent [unlock/open/untie * | $]) (bike refusal)

(bike refusal) You’re not ready to leave yet, and you’re also not ready to bring a bike any closer to a delicate archaeological site.

#backpack
(name *) backpack
(your *)
(descr *)
	Very large and very sturdy
	(if) (* is open) (then)
		(collect $Item)
			*($Item is #in *)
		(into $List)
		. (A $List) (is $List) packed inside.
	(else)
		, and currently zipped up tight.
	(endif)
(container *)
(openable *)
(wearable *)
(* is #on #bike)

(appearance $ #in *) %% Let the description handle the appearances

#waterbottle %% Not useful, but shows that you can’t carry things into dreams
(name *) water bottle
(item *)
(* is #in #backpack)
(potable *)
(descr *) Even if you’re not trekking through the desert, the temperature in Mosul is well over 30 at night. Passing out from dehydration won’t get your thesis finished.

(instead of [open/empty *]) (try [drink *])
(instead of [fill * | $]) There’s nothing else around to fill it from. Better make it last.
(narrate drinking *) You take a judiciously-measured drink. Compared to the air around you, it’s still nice and cold.

%% Book and rope are also in backpack: see systems.dg

#abovedig
(name *) above the dig site
(dict *) origin
(heads *) above
(room *)
(look *) From this upper rim you can see the whole excavation spread out below you, marked out with (zlink {string}) into a chessboard of squares. A (zlink {metal post}) marks the origin point of the whole thing.

(from * go #south to #realcamp) ~(real camp explained)
(from * go #north to #nabiyunus)
(from * go #west to #camp)
(from * go #east through #rope-u to #dig) (upper rope connected)
(from * go #east to #dig) ~(upper rope connected) %% Prevented
(from * go #down to #east)

(prevent [leave * #east])
	~(upper rope connected)
	~{ (#cart is #in #dig) (#cart is locked) }
	This ledge is about three meters above the dig site—farther than you’re willing to jump. You’ll need some way to climb down.

#post
(name *) survey post
(dict *) metal datum
(* is #in #abovedig)
(descr *) This metal post marks the datum: the origin of the archaeologists’ coordinate system. It’s anchored very securely into the ground—if it moved even a few centimeters, after all, every single measurement would need to be redone.

#grid
(name *) grid of string
(dict *) strings square squares nail nails
(descr *) Nails are set into the ground every ten meters, north and south and east and west \( and in some cases up and down \). A grid of strings between them divides the whole site into squares.

(#abovedig/#dig/#looseearth/#deeppit attracts *)

#realcamp
(name *) official camp
(room *)
(look *) You should never be here! Please report this bug!

(prevent entering *)
	Better not. It’s not like you’re a looter, or you’re not supposed to be here, or something like that...you have all the right paperwork, you’re officially a guest of the archaeologists, and besides, it’s not like you’re going to be (emph {taking}) anything. It’s just so much easier to do these things at 2:00 in the morning when you don’t have to explain what a forensic necromancer is or why you need silence to meditate over the bones.
	(now) (real camp explained) %% Remove the map connection

